I'll implement both iterative and recursive variants of binary search that find the first occurrence of a target in a sorted array.

```js
/**
 * Binary search that returns the index of the first occurrence of target in a sorted array.
 * @param {Array<number>} sorted - The sorted array to search in (ascending order)
 * @param {number} target - The value to search for
 * @returns {number} The index of the first occurrence of target, or -1 if not found
 * 
 * Requirements:
 * - Time complexity: O(log n)
 * - Space complexity: O(1)
 * - Returns first occurrence when duplicates exist
 * - Does not mutate the input array
 * - Uses safe midpoint computation: left + Math.floor((right - left) / 2)
 * 
 * Note: The input array must be sorted in ascending order for correct results.
 * When target is absent, returns -1.
 */
function binarySearch(sorted, target) {
  if (!Array.isArray(sorted)) {
    throw new TypeError('Input must be an array');
  }

  let left = 0;
  let right = sorted.length - 1;
  let result = -1;

  while (left <= right) {
    const mid = left + Math.floor((right - left) / 2);

    if (sorted[mid] === target) {
      result = mid;
      right = mid - 1; // Continue searching to the left for first occurrence
    } else if (sorted[mid] < target) {
      left = mid + 1;
    } else {
      right = mid - 1;
    }
  }

  return result;
}

/**
 * Recursive binary search that returns the index of the first occurrence of target in a sorted array.
 * @param {Array<number>} sorted - The sorted array to search in (ascending order)
 * @param {number} target - The value to search for
 * @param {number} [left=0] - Internal parameter (start of search range)
 * @param {number} [right] - Internal parameter (end of search range)
 * @returns {number} The index of the first occurrence of target, or -1 if not found
 * 
 * Requirements:
 * - Time complexity: O(log n)
 * - Space complexity: O(log n) due to recursion stack
 * - Returns first occurrence when duplicates exist
 * - Does not mutate the input array
 * - Uses safe midpoint computation: left + Math.floor((right - left) / 2)
 * - Safe for null/undefined input (validation in function body)
 * 
 * Note: The input array must be sorted in ascending order for correct results.
 * When target is absent, returns -1.
 */
function binarySearchRecursive(sorted, target, left = 0, right) {
  if (!Array.isArray(sorted)) {
    throw new TypeError('Input must be an array');
  }

  // Initialize right if not provided (first call)
  if (right === undefined) {
    right = sorted.length - 1;
  }

  // Base case: search range is exhausted
  if (left > right) {
    return -1;
  }

  const mid = left + Math.floor((right - left) / 2);

  if (sorted[mid] === target) {
    // Check if this is the first occurrence
    if (mid === 0 || sorted[mid - 1] < target) {
      return mid;
    } else {
      // Continue searching to the left
      return binarySearchRecursive(sorted, target, left, mid - 1);
    }
  } else if (sorted[mid] < target) {
    // Search right half
    return binarySearchRecursive(sorted, target, mid + 1, right);
  } else {
    // Search left half
    return binarySearchRecursive(sorted, target, left, mid - 1);
  }
}
```

### Implementation Details:

**Iterative Approach:**
- Time Complexity: O(log n) - The search space is halved with each iteration
- Space Complexity: O(1) - Only a few variables are used regardless of input size
- When finding the target, it continues searching to the left (by setting `right = mid - 1`) to ensure it finds the first occurrence
- Uses safe midpoint calculation: `left + Math.floor((right - left) / 2)` to prevent potential overflow

**Recursive Approach:**
- Time Complexity: O(log n) - Same as iterative approach
- Space Complexity: O(log n) - Due to the recursion stack depth
- Includes validation for null/undefined input in the function body
- When finding the target, it checks if it's the first occurrence by verifying the element to the left is different
- If not the first occurrence, it continues searching to the left

**Boundary Cases:**
- Empty array: Returns -1
- Single element array: Returns 0 if it matches the target, otherwise -1
- All elements are the same: Returns 0 (first occurrence)
- Target not present: Returns -1
- Target is smaller than all elements: Returns -1
- Target is larger than all elements: Returns -1

Both implementations maintain the requirement to not mutate or sort the input array and correctly handle arrays with duplicates by returning the first occurrence of the target.

